% ============================================================================
% landforms — the common landforms and the short defining descriptor its
% source states for each (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of physical geography, and a rung a GEOGRAPHY or
% EARTH-SCIENCE agent reasons UP from: the solid surface of the Earth is not
% uniform — it is built out of a small set of recurring shapes, and each shape
% has a characteristic defining feature. "A mountain projects conspicuously
% above its surroundings; a valley is low land bordered by higher ground; a
% plateau is a flat area of great elevation; a plain is comparatively level;
% a canyon is narrow and deep." Before you can reason about where a river runs,
% where rain falls, or where a road can climb, you must first know WHICH
% landform you are standing on. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? landform_description(canyon, $Desc)      % deep_narrow
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `landform_description(landform, description)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the descriptor WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these landforms (it never invents a
% descriptor).
%
% Each common landform and the short defining descriptor the source states for
% it, as a little truth table:
%
%     landform    defining descriptor          a beginner's cue
%     --------    -------------------------     --------------------------------
%     mountain    projects_above_surroundings   stands conspicuously above the land
%     valley      low_between_mountains          low land, often between two mountains
%     plateau     flat_elevated                  a flat area, but held up high
%     plain       comparatively_level            broad, comparatively level ground
%     canyon      deep_narrow                    a narrow, deep, steep-sided cut
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a descriptor and recall its landform:
%
%     ? landform_description($Landform, deep_narrow)   % canyon
%
% A word that is NOT one of these landforms — `ocean`, `island`, `river` — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a landform recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a descriptor. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the landform→descriptor
% pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `description` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be
% a defining descriptor the source EXPLICITLY states for that landform — never
% one the source does not support. Each atom uses the source's own words (the
% USGS thesaurus says "narrow, deep", not "gorge"; it says "project ... above
% their surroundings", not "tall"), so every value stays literally checkable
% against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every landform→descriptor
% pair below was read out of ONE fetched USGS-hosted source, the Alexandria
% Digital Library Feature Type Thesaurus served at apps.usgs.gov, and any pair
% whose descriptor could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each
% pair, with the verbatim span that states it (all on the one thesaurus page):
%
%   mountain → projects_above_surroundings
%     "Landmasses that project conspicuously above their surroundings."
%
%   valley → low_between_mountains
%     "Low-lying land bordered by higher ground; especially elongate,
%      relatively large gently sloping depressions of the Earth's surface,
%      commonly situated between two mountains or between ranges of hills or
%      mountains, and often containing a stream with an outlet."
%
%   plateau → flat_elevated
%     "Comparatively flat areas of great extent and elevation; specif.
%      extensive land regions considerably above the adjacent country or above
%      sea level; commonly limited on at least one side by an abrupt descent ..."
%
%   plain → comparatively_level
%     "Regions of general uniform slope, comparatively level and of
%      considerable extent."
%
%   canyon → deep_narrow
%     "Relatively narrow, deep depressions with steep sides, the bottom of
%      which generally has a continuous slope."
%
% DROPPED — `hill`: this authoritative source does not carry a standalone
% `hills` definition (the word appears only as a synonym folded under the
% `mountains` entry). Rather than borrow a descriptor from a weaker source and
% drag the whole table down to a lower trust tier, the hill row is dropped, per
% the honest-drop discipline.
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the thesaurus statement that
% fixes the first row (mountain → projects_above_surroundings). Every OTHER
% row's verbatim span is listed above, and all of them live on the SAME
% USGS-hosted thesaurus page named in `locator`, so each descriptor remains
% independently checkable. The source is served by the U.S. Geological Survey
% (a primary U.S. government resource), so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table landform_description {
    columns landform, description

    row (mountain, projects_above_surroundings)
    row (valley, low_between_mountains)
    row (plateau, flat_elevated)
    row (plain, comparatively_level)
    row (canyon, deep_narrow)

    source "Landmasses that project conspicuously above their surroundings."
    locator "https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=3"
    trust authoritative
}
